DSA Notes · Two Pointers

Reverse a string, in place
Walk two fingers in from both ends and swap as they pass. My notes on the idea, the one tricky bit, the code, and how to read the constraints.

Two-pointer
In-place · returns void
Time O(n)
Space O(1)
LeetCode 344
01
The problem, in my words
What am I actually being asked to do?

I'm given an array of characters and I have to reverse it — but not by building a new reversed array. I have to change the same array. That's why the function returns nothing (void / None): the "answer" is the array itself, edited where it sits.

So the whole game is: rearrange the existing slots. First character should end up last, last should end up first, and everything meets in the middle.

02
The idea — two pointers
One finger at the start, one at the end, walking toward each other.

Put a left pointer at index 0 and a right pointer at the last index. Swap the two characters they point to, then step left forward and right backward. Repeat.

I keep going while left < right. The moment they meet or cross, every character has already been moved — so I stop. (In example one the right index starts at 3; in example two it starts at 6. The rule is the same: stop when the fingers touch.)

