03
The twist I found
Why a swap needs a third variable.

Here's the bit that tripped me up. To swap two slots I can't just do a = b then b = a — the first line overwrites a, so the second line just copies the value back and both slots end up the same. The original value of a is gone.

The fix — a temp variable
Stash one value in a third variable first, then it's safe to overwrite. left → temp, then right → left, then temp → right. Nothing is lost. (Python lets you shortcut this with s[l], s[r] = s[r], s[l] — but a hidden temp is doing exactly this behind the scenes.)

04
The code
Python — both the explicit-temp version and the clean one.

reverse_string.py
class Solution:
    def reverseString(self, s: list[str]) -> None:
        # modify s in place, return nothing
        left, right = 0, len(s) - 1

        while left < right:
            # --- swap using a temp (the twist) ---
            temp    = s[left]
            s[left] = s[right]
            s[right] = temp

            # walk the pointers inward
            left  += 1
            right -= 1

        # Pythonic one-liner for the same swap:
        # s[left], s[right] = s[right], s[left]
05
Time & space
Solve first, then account for the cost — like I planned.

Time
O(n)
I touch each character once. It's really n/2 swaps, but we drop constants — so O(n).

Space
O(1)
Only left, right, and one temp. No second array — the size doesn't grow with the input.

06
Reading the constraints
What Big-O means, and how input size hints at the algorithm.

What does Big-O actually mean? Think of n as the size of the input, and Big-O as roughly how the work grows as n grows. O(n) means "work grows in step with the input"; O(n²) means "double the input, quadruple the work."
  
The size limits in a problem are a hint about which complexity will survive. Rough guide:

Input size (n)	Usually think about
n ≤ 10	Almost anything — even O(n!) / brute force
n ≤ 20	Backtracking / exponential may be possible
n ≤ 100	O(n²) often fine
n ≤ 1,000	O(n²) often okay
n ≤ 10,000	O(n²) may be risky
n ≤ 100,000	Prefer O(n log n) or O(n)
n ≤ 1,000,000	Usually O(n) / O(log n)
Not strict laws — rough guidelines. When in doubt, aim one tier faster than "just okay."

